%description:

RFC 1122 -- the datagram that waits for a resolution is saved and sent after the reply.

Checks RFC1122-AQUEUE-1 (should) from doc/project/evidence/standard/rfc1122/catalog.md, and
covers RFC826-REQ-3, which it overrides. The English procedure is in
doc/project/evidence/protocol/arp/checks/queue.md#the-waiting-datagram; the program steps
below map onto its expected observations. Mapping of the pair mockup: host A = hostA,
host B = hostB.

Host A sends exactly one datagram, and nothing sends it again. The two documents predict two
different outcomes for that one datagram: RFC 826 throws it away, so it never arrives, and
RFC 1122 saves it, so it arrives after the reply. One observation therefore tells the two
apart, and a program that retransmitted would hide the difference.

The order of the steps is the order of the expected observations, so the step that observes
the datagram cannot match before the step that observes the reply has matched. "After the
reply and not before it" is the step order itself.

The last step watches the handoff from UDP to the program, which is where the datagram
"reaches the program" of the check. The UDP header is already gone at that point, so the
step reads the length of the data, 100 octets, and only one datagram is in flight.

%includes:
#include "ProtocolTest.h"
#include "ArpMutations.h"

%global:

using namespace inet;
using namespace inet::protocoltest;

Define_ProtocolTestProgram()
{
    return ProtocolTest("rfc1122_arp_packet_queue")
        // Expected observation 1: the datagram could not go out, and the resolution started.
        .once(on("hostA.eth[0].mac").signal("packetSentToLower")
                  .packet("arp.opcode == 1")
                  .describe("the request from host A")
                  .within(0.5))
        // Expected observation 2: host B answers.
        .once(on("hostB.eth[0].mac").signal("packetSentToLower")
                  .packet("arp.opcode == 2")
                  .describe("host B's reply")
                  .within(0.1))
        // Expected observation 3: the one datagram reaches the program on host B, after the
        // reply (RFC1122-AQUEUE-1).
        .once(on("hostB.udp").signal("packetSentToUpper")
                  .match([](const MatchContext& c) { return c.event.packet->getDataLength() == B(100); })
                  .describe("the saved datagram handed up to the program on host B, after the reply")
                  .within(0.5));
}

%activity:
// present only so opp_test compiles the %global above; this module is not in the network.
EV << "rfc1122_arp_packet_queue compiled\n";

%file: test.ned

import inet.node.ethernet.Eth100M;
import inet.node.inet.StandardHost;
import inet.networklayer.configurator.ipv4.Ipv4NetworkConfigurator;

// The pair mockup: two hosts on one Ethernet link.
network ArpPairNet
{
    submodules:
        configurator: Ipv4NetworkConfigurator { @display("p=100,50"); }
        hostA: StandardHost { @display("p=300,100"); }
        hostB: StandardHost { @display("p=500,100"); }
    connections:
        hostA.ethg++ <--> Eth100M <--> hostB.ethg++; // link 1
}

%inifile: omnetpp.ini

[General]
network = ArpPairNet
sim-time-limit = 1s
seed-set = 0

# Exactly one datagram of 100 octets, and nothing sends it again.
*.hostA.numApps = 1
*.hostA.app[0].typename = "UdpBasicApp"
*.hostA.app[0].destAddresses = "hostB"
*.hostA.app[0].destPort = 5000
*.hostA.app[0].localPort = 4000
*.hostA.app[0].messageLength = 100B
*.hostA.app[0].startTime = 0.1s
*.hostA.app[0].sendInterval = 1s
*.hostA.app[0].stopTime = 0.15s
*.hostB.numApps = 1
*.hostB.app[0].typename = "UdpSink"
*.hostB.app[0].localPort = 5000

%contains: stdout
PROTOCOLTEST rfc1122_arp_packet_queue: PASS
